## The message that arrived on time

Remember the last time you got a message saying “Your booking is confirmed” or “See you tomorrow at 10:00”. No person wrote it: a service sent it by itself because you did something. That is **marketing automation**: messages go out by themselves, in response to what people do, by rules you described in advance.

Why would a project without a marketing department want it? Three reasons:

- **You can’t answer everyone on time**, and a person who sent a request expects an answer today, not on Friday.
- **People decide slowly.** In the lessons on the funnel we said that days pass between “found out” and “decided”. An automatic chain accompanies the person through those days for you.
- **A rule that is written down can be improved.** In the lessons on the value ladder you invented a lead magnet and a low-priced first offer. A flow is what delivers them to the person and leads to the next step.

**Where it comes from.** The idea is old: direct marketing by post long had series of letters sent by rules. Software for it appeared in the 1990s alongside the first customer relationship systems, and broad platforms such as HubSpot and Marketo grew in the 2000s. Today the English-speaking web has dozens of email and automation services (Mailchimp, Klaviyo, ConvertKit and many others), and messengers have their own families of bot builders. Russia has its own services too, which we leave aside. We don’t recommend any of them: what matters is the logic, which is the same everywhere. Before choosing a service, check where it stores people’s data (we’ll say more in the lesson on email and in the part on law).

## Four building blocks

Any flow is put together from four parts.

1. **The trigger.** The event that starts the flow: the person sent a form, subscribed to a channel, booked, downloaded a checklist, opened the pricing page and left. The best trigger is the **main action from the brief**, or a step toward it.
2. **The condition** (“if”). A check on which what happens depends: did the person open the email, which segment are they in, have they already booked.
3. **The pause.** The wait between steps: “after two days”. A pause respects the rhythm of the person’s decision. Its length depends on how urgent the decision is: a consultation about a boundary can wait a few days, but you can’t book tomorrow’s appointment after a week.
4. **The message.** What goes to the person, in the right channel. A channel is an email, a text message, a message in a messenger or a direct message on a social network. Each message should have **one goal**.

Three goals are enough for almost any small project:

- **Remind**: “here is what you did and what happens next”;
- **Prove**: “here is an example of how it goes”, that is, the material from the lessons on proof and case studies;
- **Offer**: “here is the next step”, one action, without pressure.

And two ideas without which any talk of flows is incomplete.

A **segment** is a group of people with a shared feature, for whom the flow is the same. You met segmentation in the lesson on STP. Here a segment is built from what you know about the person: where they came from, what they downloaded, what they chose in the form. A flow “for everyone” usually speaks to no one, so a small site needs only two or three segments, and the first is the one written in the “for whom” field of your brief.

A **cascade** (also called a drip campaign) is a chain of several messages, each sent after a pause and perhaps after a check of a condition. The diagram below shows a cascade of three messages.

The **exit condition** is a separate and very important rule: when the flow stops for this person. Most often it is “took the action”, “replied” or “unsubscribed”.

**An example with the default persona (the invented Kate Alder).** The trigger: a person sent a request for a consultation. The segment: private landowners getting in touch for the first time. Day 0, an email: “Request received, here is what to prepare and when I’ll call” (remind). Day 2, a text message, if the person agreed to texts: a short walk through a typical boundary situation (prove). Day 5, an email: “If your question is still open, here are times for a consultation” (offer). The exit: the person booked, replied or unsubscribed.

## The cascade in action

Open the diagram. Three invented people go through the same cascade of three messages. Three switches are three rules: a pause, an exit condition, and a second message by text for those who didn’t open the email. Switch the rules off one at a time and see which mistakes appear.

<CascadeBuilder />

The third switch is especially useful. Vera agreed to email only, so a text message to her must not be sent, even if the flow “wants” to. Automation does not know by itself what a person has allowed. You know it, and the rule has to be written into the flow.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: which two parts of a flow protect a person from too many messages?</summary>

The pause (so the messages don’t arrive in a heap) and the exit condition (so the flow stops when the goal is reached). A third safeguard is the check of consent for each channel.

</details>

## Consent: a guideline, not legal advice

It is very easy to get this wrong, so briefly and carefully. What follows is how lawyers commonly read the rules, not advice on your own case, and it is worth checking with a lawyer.

- **A message with an offer and a message “in reply to your request” are not the same.** A booking confirmation, a reminder of a consultation the person booked, and a message with a document they asked for are usually not marketing. Emails with new offers and invitations are.
- **In the US,** the CAN-SPAM Act lets you send marketing email without prior consent, but each message must say who sends it and where they are (a real postal address), must not mislead in its header or subject, and must give a clear way to opt out, which you honor within ten business days. Transactional or relationship messages (a confirmation, a receipt) are mostly exempt from these rules. **Marketing texts are stricter:** under the Telephone Consumer Protection Act, automated marketing texts to a mobile number need the person’s prior express written consent, and a ticked box or a reply to a keyword can count when the wording is clear. State laws add their own rules.
- **In the UK and the EU,** the rule is the reverse: marketing email to an individual needs prior consent (an opt-in), except for the “soft opt-in”, where you got the person’s address in the course of a sale, you market similar products, and you offered an opt-out when you collected it and in every message (PECR regulation 22 in the UK, Article 13 of the ePrivacy Directive in the EU). Consent as GDPR defines it has to be freely given, specific, informed and unambiguous, and a person can always object to direct marketing.
- **In Canada,** the anti-spam law (CASL) asks for express or implied consent before a commercial electronic message. We mention it only so you know that the rules differ between countries: if your audience is there, read the regulator’s page.
- **Consent is counted per channel.** Email and text message are two different consents.

So for every flow you describe, write down: **for which channel, when and how the person consented**, and how they can withdraw. If you can’t answer, the message must not be sent. Which law applies depends on where your recipients are, not only on where you are. This is general information, not legal advice: for a real flow ask a lawyer, or the regulator (the FTC and the FCC in the US, the ICO in the UK, your national data protection authority in the EU).

## What to measure in a flow

It’s tempting to look at the “open rate”. But since 2021, mail apps with privacy protection (Apple Mail became the well-known example) load in advance the images by which an “open” is detected, so there are more opens than readers. Three numbers are more reliable:

- **how many people reached each message** (didn’t leave earlier);
- **how many pressed the button or link** in the message;
- **how many took the target action** for which it was all started.

How to tag links and count clicks you’ll learn in the lesson on social networks (UTM tags) and in the part on metrics. And in the lesson on the funnel you saw that every step of a funnel loses people: a cascade helps lose fewer.

## Where automation stops

- **Don’t automate judgment.** Automate the booking confirmation, but not the answer to “what do I do about my boundary?”, especially if you are an expert and answer for the words.
- **Don’t pass a machine off as a person.** “Written just for you” from a robot is deceit. Honest is a signature such as “an automatic message, reply to it and a person will answer”.
- **Keep the flow short.** Three to five messages are enough. Ten emails in a “series” is already pressure, not support.
- **Review it once a month.** A flow that once sent a price list, when the prices have since changed, does harm.
- **Follow the law.** The rules on mailings and personal data have changed more than once and will change again. What this lesson says is only a guideline.

## Practice

Describe a flow for your project’s main action.

1. **The trigger**: what the person did for the flow to start. The main action from the brief, in the same words.
2. **The segment**: who the flow is for. The “for whom” wording from the brief and a note on what makes the segment special.
3. **Three messages**: when (with the pause in numbers), in which channel (at least two different ones), with what goal (remind, prove, offer) and about what.
4. **The exit condition** and **the consent**: for which channel and how the person gave it.

The check looks at form: the trigger and segment from the brief, three messages, pauses in numbers, two channels, three goals, an exit and a consent. Whether the flow is good is for you to decide. Picture a person from “for whom” who has no time: will each message reach them on time, and do they need it?
